# Terraform state holds the database password in CLEARTEXT.
#
# `db_password` is marked sensitive in variables.tf, which controls whether
# Terraform prints it — not whether it stores it. The kubernetes_secret
# resource records POSTGRES_PASSWORD under `data` in plain text, and
# .tfstate.backup keeps the previous copy, so a `terraform destroy` does not
# clean it up. Committing either file publishes the credential.
#
# This is the one file in the repo that exists to prevent a mistake rather than
# to enable something.
terraform.tfstate
terraform.tfstate.*
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*

# Plan files embed variable values, including the password.
*.tfplan
tfplan*

# Variable files: the repo passes db_password from a Harness secret, so there
# should be no reason to have these. If one appears locally, it is holding
# something that does not belong in git.
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json

# Provider binaries and local backend scratch. .terraform.lock.hcl is NOT
# ignored — that one belongs in git, so every run resolves the same provider
# versions.
.terraform/
.terraform.tfstate.lock.info

# The real backend config, copied from backend-kubernetes.tf.example by the
# provision stage. Ignored so a local apply keeps using local state.
backend-kubernetes.tf

.DS_Store
BLOG.md
